#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" :question/math-academy-id "e-5424" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n![](images/ac/ac61fea55da2b0f5b4c2b21b8915c7eb7fc30c576b2ca7dea830ae0aa9183dd6.png)\n\n- $A$ is not invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n![](images/f5/f57560debe62c432de44b86e2b1f569b703b9ef3faae46bcc2df18cca17a2c62.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n![](images/65/65f646a200575b7299fc0278f7a5dc73cba98e293943bdb592ca24c4dd01df0f.png)\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\n- $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](images/7a/7ab0bb5d64ace5e31ea02d022aa0d8696deb3b62555cfccac069ba74ff750526.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" :question/math-academy-id "e-9962" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0ab76d251e530205b2fad22f1b4a4677370459e5cd7e19da4220c7c4baac51a3.png)\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/c9/c9e4725a19de7c9a07cec4b929905c3d1907871b46deb3ab197b7481aa2a0467.png)\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8cade90-de90-5cc5-a96f-4b62957e1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121332} {:answer/id #uuid "fc71a2e9-4828-5b92-b019-af28e15c72d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121333} {:answer/id #uuid "3bff6895-2d99-520f-a92f-efa5cdf98ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Col(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121334} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1a034d-0611-5f21-a47f-fb41d7d10986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121335} {:answer/id #uuid "7c678610-40fd-5c56-a8a7-b4790367a825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121336}] :answer-field/id #uuid "36cf5fde-aae3-5ac2-808d-cd031ad46138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121331}] :question/id #uuid "b33961fd-1191-51c9-841c-0bdd04b0318b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140766" :question/problem "$Ais invertible⇔{Col(A) = R^{n}; dim(Col(A)) = n = rank(A); Null(A) = W; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\cdot n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10dd7d25-8cfc-52a1-acc2-3277c8eeb2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(I-{A}^{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269419} {:answer/id #uuid "22f60b50-9d54-5d0c-bd92-26d69595fd5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269574} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc3a383-4f66-5f23-a4b0-402cfefdcb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270689} {:answer/id #uuid "abdc186b-e8ae-5ad6-90c7-9f9264cfc7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{-1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270802} {:answer/id #uuid "fd18314a-6226-54bc-8252-cb6f9e346896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det({A}^{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fc3a383-4f66-5f23-a4b0-402cfefdcb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270689} :answer-field/id #uuid "34c48b4c-89fa-5a1f-9048-8c8927a71839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d5626a2-9938-5097-90ed-3e9019e50489" :question/math-academy-id "q-142357" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & ={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Col}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c513965-3717-5fb0-bff9-e3b6e5bcd203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327461} {:answer/id #uuid "47d7f51f-15b0-533b-9e9b-82e13d4120ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327559} {:answer/id #uuid "729c1473-5c2d-5ba8-ab43-6594924aadab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327926} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a3d425-a91c-5c69-83f8-fb2c7b092295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328366} {:answer/id #uuid "d140b502-8ea3-591c-b1b5-620e9e563979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d140b502-8ea3-591c-b1b5-620e9e563979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328773} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e66aa9-8412-5e42-8df8-d0eb8bcf68b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c600e13-fdff-5526-82d0-6659ac10dfc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-47587" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $A$ has no inverse\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n- All columns of $A$ are pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times $\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
